{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>The day of Pharaoh's birth.</b> The day of his birth, and they call it the day of genesisya. And the form הֻלֶּדֶת [hophal]: because the infant is not formed except through others — that the midwife delivers the woman, and therefore the midwife is called מְיַלֶּדֶת. So too ״וּמוֹלְדוֹתַיִךְ בְּיוֹם הוּלֶּדֶת אוֹתָךְ״ (Ezekiel 16), and so too ״אַחֲרֵי הֻכַּבֵּס אֶת הַנֶּגַע״ (Leviticus 13) — because its washing is through others." "<b>And he lifted the head of, etc.</b> He counted them together with the rest of his servants — that he would count the attendants who served him at his feast, and he recalled these among them, as in ״שְׂאוּ אֶת רֹאשׁ״ (Numbers 1) — a term of counting."], :book :genesis, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 40, :notes {0 "גֵּינוּסְיָא is the Greek-derived term gensia/genesia, a birthday feast."}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:02:52.353332013Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}